//? Title: Graham scan: sort the rays, remove the right turn
//? Subtitle: Actual native stack edits, exact binary64 predicates and selected work
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
The convex hull is the smallest convex set containing a finite planar point
cloud. Graham scan first orders rays around an extreme point, then keeps a stack
whose boundary turns left. We begin with one actual removal from a five-point
example, derive the geometric invariant and linear scan bound, and retain the
original twenty-point cloud. Native records expose the producing angular order,
input identities and stack edits without replay. Exact orientation signs avoid
rounded-product failures, while explicit controls cover duplicate, collinear,
small and invalid inputs. Work counts describe selected operations, not elapsed
speed.
)

\toc

> See one removal before the full algorithm

Take the pivot (0,0), then rays through (4,0), (3,1), (4,4) and (0,4). When
(4,4) arrives, the current top (3,1) bends the path right. Removing that point
connects (4,0) directly to (4,4). The following program selects the first actual
pop and the test immediately preceding it; it does not reconstruct a second
scan.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/graham_turn.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const auto t =
        dm::geometry::graham_scan_trace({{0, 0}, {4, 0}, {3, 1}, {4, 4}, {0, 4}});
    std::print("| order | original row | x | y |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.ordered.size(); ++i) {
        const auto &p = t.ordered[i];
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", i, p.input_index, p.point.x,
                   p.point.y);
    }
    std::print("\n| event | action | candidate | edited point | exact turn | stack size after |\n| ---: | " "--- | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: |\n");
    const char *names[] = {"test", "pop", "push"};
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.events.size(); ++i) {
        const auto &e = t.events[i];
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", i,
                   names[std::size_t(e.action)], e.candidate, e.point,
                   e.action == dm::geometry::HullAction::test
                   ? std::to_string(e.turn) : "none", e.stack_size);
    }
    const auto pop =
        std::find_if(t.events.begin(), t.events.end(), [](const auto &e) {
        return e.action == dm::geometry::HullAction::pop;
    });
    if (pop == t.events.end() || pop == t.events.begin())
        throw std::runtime_error("pop witness");
    const auto &e = *(pop - 1);
    const auto a =
        t.ordered[e.a].point, b =
        t.ordered[e.b].point, c = t.ordered[e.candidate].point;
    dm::graphics::Diagram d(115, 950);
    dm::graphics::DiagramStroke red;
    red.color = {220, 38, 38};
    red.width = 3;
    red.dashed = true;
    dm::graphics::DiagramStroke blue;
    blue.color = {37, 99, 235};
    blue.width = 3;
    d.path({{a.y, -a.x}, {b.y, -b.x}, {c.y, -c.x}}, red).path(
        {{a.y, -a.x}, {c.y, -c.x}}, blue);
    for (auto p : {a, b, c})
        d.ellipse({p.y, -p.x}, .065, .065, {30, 41, 59});
    d.text({a.y, -a.x - .5}, std::format("a = ({},{})", a.x, a.y), 23).text(
        {b.y, -b.x + .5}, std::format("pop b = ({},{})", b.x, b.y), 23).text(
        {c.y, -c.x - .5}, std::format("p = ({},{})", c.x, c.y), 23).text(
        {2, -1.6}, std::format("Actual right turn: exact sign {}", e.turn), 25).text({2, -5.3}, "Dashed: old two edges; blue: surviving edge", 22).save_svg("build/graham_turn.svg");
    if (e.turn != -1 || t.analysis.hull.size() != 4)
        throw std::runtime_error("actual right turn");
}
```
