> Derive the orientation and stack invariant

For points \(a,b,p\), the determinant is twice their signed triangle area:

$$\Delta(a,b,p)=(b_x-a_x)(p_y-a_y)-(b_y-a_y)(p_x-a_x).$$
\alttext(The orientation determinant is the horizontal first displacement times the vertical second displacement, minus the vertical first displacement times the horizontal second displacement.)

Positive means left, negative right, and zero exactly collinear. Here
\(\Delta((4,0),(3,1),(4,4))=-4\), so the middle point cannot remain on the
strict hull boundary. The native predicate returns only the exact sign for the
stored finite binary64 coordinates, using fixed integer arithmetic rather than
subtracting rounded floating products. It does not recover unknown unrounded
measurements.

Preparation sorts coordinates lexicographically and removes duplicates,
retaining the earliest original row. For at least three distinct points, choose
the minimum y, then minimum x, as pivot. Every other point lies in its closed
upper half-plane, with any horizontal ray pointing right. Consequently, the
orientation sign orders rays by angle without an atan2 calculation or a
wraparound discontinuity. Equal-angle points go near to far: increasing y, then
x, suffices on this restricted set of rays and avoids squared-distance overflow.

As each candidate arrives, the stack is the convex boundary chain for the
processed angular prefix. While its last triple is nonpositive, pop the top.
With increasing ray angles, a right-turn middle point lies inside the boundary
completed by the new candidate; a zero-turn middle point lies on an edge and is
omitted from the strict vertex list. Each removal restores the only possibly
broken end of the chain. Pushing the candidate preserves the invariant. At
completion the two extreme rays join through the pivot, yielding the
counterclockwise hull without repeating its first vertex.

Duplicates, all-collinear inputs and fewer-than-three shortcuts need explicit
treatment; a claim that every returned list is a polygon would be incorrect.

> Preserve the original twenty-point cloud

This fixed cloud has eight hull vertices. Earliest original row IDs accompany
their coordinates. The numerical plot uses a square data region and equal
coordinate spans, so a unit on x has the same displayed length as a unit on y.
The monotone-chain comparison is another native algorithm sharing the exact
predicate; independent supporting-line tests provide separate verification.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/graham_cloud.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const auto points = dm::geometry::graham_demo_points();
    const auto a = dm::geometry::graham_scan_analyze(points);
    std::print("| hull order | original row | x | y |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < a.hull.size(); ++i)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", i, a.input_indices[i],
                   a.hull[i].x, a.hull[i].y);
    auto other = dm::geometry::convex_hull(points);
    auto hull = a.hull;
    const auto lex = [](const auto &x, const auto &y) {
        return x.x < y.x || (x.x == y.x && x.y < y.y);
    };
    std::sort(other.begin(), other.end(), lex);
    std::sort(hull.begin(), hull.end(), lex);
    const auto &w = a.work;
    std::print("\n{} input rows; {} distinct points; {} hull vertices; {} angular tests; {} sweep tests; {} " "pushes; {} pops. Same vertex set as native monotone chain: {}.\n",
               w.input_points, w.distinct_points, a.hull.size(),
               w.angular_tests, w.sweep_tests, w.pushes, w.pops, other == hull);
    dm::geometry::graham_hull_plot(points, a).save_svg("build/graham_cloud.svg");
    if (a.hull.size() != 8 || other != hull)
        throw std::runtime_error("original cloud");
}
