> Count the scan separately from its sorting

Let \(n\) be the original row count, \(m\) the distinct count and \(h\) the hull
size. There are two sorting phases: lexicographic preparation costs
\(O(n\log n)\) comparisons, and polar sorting costs \(O(m\log m)\) exact
orientation requests. Deduplication and pivot selection are linear. The stack
sweep pushes every distinct point once and pops each discarded point once:

$$P=m,\qquad R=m-h,\qquad T=R+S\le(m-h)+(m-2)\le2m-4\quad(m\ge3).$$
\alttext(There are m pushes and m minus h pops. Sweep tests consist of pop-causing tests plus successful left-turn tests; at most m minus two tests can succeed, giving a bound of two m minus four.)

The first two candidates need no orientation test. Every later candidate
produces at most one successful test, while every nonpositive test produces a
pop. This amortized count proves the linear sweep. A compact trace has exactly
\(P+R+T\) events, hence at most \(4m-6\); it stores edits rather than full stack
snapshots. Full computation is \(O(n\log n)\), with \(O(n)\) working storage
plus linear optional capture. Exact binary64 signs have a bounded fixed-integer
cost, which is larger than an unchecked rounded determinant; selected counts are
not timing comparisons.

The next program contrasts square clouds with all-convex integer parabola
points. Its generated inputs use the existing native point-cloud API. Square
coordinates are quantized on its documented grid; each requested size and actual
distinct count is printed. Generator ordering and standard-library sort counts
can vary across toolchains, so the bound checks concern the producing run.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/graham_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    std::print("| family | input n | distinct m | hull h | lex comparisons | angular tests | sweep tests | " "pushes | pops |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    std::vector<double> sizes, square, parabola;
    for (int n : {16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512, 1024, 2048}) {
        for (auto family : {dm::geometry::PointCloud::Square,
                            dm::geometry::PointCloud::Parabola}) {
            const auto a =
                dm::geometry::graham_scan_analyze(dm::geometry::random_point_cloud(n, family, 17));
            const auto &w = a.work;
            const bool all = family == dm::geometry::PointCloud::Parabola;
            std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",
                       all ? "parabola" : "square", n, w.distinct_points,
                       a.hull.size(), w.lexicographic_comparisons,
                       w.angular_tests, w.sweep_tests, w.pushes, w.pops);
            if (w.pushes != w.distinct_points ||
                w.pops != w.distinct_points - a.hull.size() ||
                w.sweep_tests > 2 * w.distinct_points - 4 ||
                (all && a.hull.size() != std::size_t(n)))
                throw std::runtime_error("scan bound");
            (all ? parabola : square).push_back(double(w.sweep_tests) /
                                                w.distinct_points);
        }
        sizes.push_back(n);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(sizes, square, "square", {37, 99, 235}, 2).points(sizes, square, "",
                                                             {37, 99, 235}, 4).line(sizes, parabola, "parabola", {220, 38, 38}, 2).points(sizes, parabola, "", {220, 38, 38}, 4).abline_h(2, {5, 150, 105}, 1.5).title("Actual sweep tests per distinct point").x_label("requested input rows").y_label("sweep tests / m").y_limits(.8, 2.35).save_svg("build/graham_work.svg");
}
```
