\image(figures/graham_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Each bar comes from the actual native result. The rounded determinant is shown separately from the exact orientation sign; the near-collinear triangle retains three vertices.)
\alttext(Six bars give hull sizes zero, one, two, two, four and three for the printed controls. A near-collinear triangle remains nondegenerate under exact signs.)

The new analyze/trace APIs default to a complete original-point cap of one
million, counting duplicate rows too. The trace additionally admits actual whole
test/pop/push records online, default one million; zero disables either policy
cap. All coordinates are validated before indexed allocation or cap checks. The
legacy by-value API's argument copy happens before the function body, and it has
no optional caps. Limits exclude ordinary result/work state, capacities and
byte/peak-memory costs. No partial trace escapes a failure.

Owned indices retain the earliest source row; copied results and histories are
independent. The legacy counted overload adds only angular plus sweep
orientation requests, after successful completion, and checks signed-counter
overflow. Sorting counts vary with the standard library. Independent calls share
no mutable state; callers must not concurrently mutate borrowed inputs.

> Evidence and limits

A hull answers an enclosure question about the supplied coordinates, not a
density or outlier-robustness question. One extreme observation can enlarge it
substantially. Synthetic point sets are appropriate here: they isolate turn
removal, edge exclusion, degeneracy and scan growth without attaching an
unrelated dataset.

R. L. Graham's [1972 primary
paper](https://doi.org/10.1016/0020-0190(72)90045-2), Information Processing
Letters 1, 132–133, establishes angular ordering followed by linear elimination.
The paper uses an interior reference point and a cyclic formulation; this
implementation uses an extreme pivot and a stack, with explicit
duplicate/collinear policy. It is a related modern variant rather than a literal
transcription. Exact predicate behavior is documented separately in the core
orientation API.

Verification checks every subset of a three-by-three integer lattice and seeded
integer clouds against an independent supporting-line oracle, reconstructs stack
events and identities, and covers full finite scales, cancellation, quotas and
ownership. A frozen pre-conversion kernel checks ordinary integer-input results
and selected orientation counts; changes near degeneracy are intentional
correctness fixes. The original hull-algorithm suite checks the caller through
Chan's group-hull path as well. All executable blocks, figures, documentation
examples and HTML/Markdown exports are checked before retiring the old report.
